From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Scott Quarrier <squarrier@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 19:31:32 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] glm5_next/cuda: shared-prefix prompt states,
 TF_GLM_SHARED_PREFIX (MiaAI-Lab recipe patches 0015, 0063)

MiaAI-Lab's recipe (github.com/MiaAI-Lab/GLM-5.3-Flash-EXL3-2x-DGX-Sparks-TensorFold @ 1576746, with
TF_GLM_SHARED_PREFIX=1 in its config) keeps, besides a prompt's own state before its last token, up to two
shared-prefix states: where the prompt stops sharing a kept prompt, and at the end of its system block (its first
<|user|>), each at least 256 tokens past the resume point. A later request that shares only that prefix (a new user
turn on the same system prompt, as llama-benchy's prefix-caching mode sends, or a new agent session) then resumes the
whole system block instead of prefilling it again. This ports recipe patch 0015-glm-shared-prefix, its multi-stream
parts from 0030-glm-multi-stream-engine, and 0063-glm-kept-cap-superseded-first (Apache License 2.0, Copyright 2026
MiaAI-Lab):

- engine.py: shared_prefix, common_prefix, shared_points and user_opener are the recipe's functions. GlmEngine._shared
  (rank 0 picks the points; two slots of the request header carry them to rank 1), _kept_drafter, and 0015's hunks of
  _run_once (each point prefilled to as a prompt of its own and kept, then resumed from; what a request resumed from
  becomes the newest), _take_over, generate and follow are applied by hand: TensorFold's engine keeps a prompt's own
  state inside a prompt chunk (prefill's keep_at), and that stays as it is.
- decode.py: _drafter_views, _saved_views, save_rows, row_bytes and load_rows are the recipe's, so saved rows keep
  DFlash2's window and a saved state still fits a DFlash2 request. prefill(sample=False) commits a prefix without
  sampling a first token (it still runs the head on its last row).
- multi.py (PR #243's engine): SHARED_SLOTS; rank 0's points ride in the ADMIT, inside its sealed message;
  Lane.shared, and lane.stops = shared + [point] in _admitted, so #243's own _fill and _keep keep those states on
  both ranks; kept states are marked shared. cap_victim (0063) past TF_GLM_CACHE_ENTRIES: a conversation's
  superseded states go first, non-shared before shared, then the least recently used.

Differences from the recipe: there is no prompt grid here (the recipe's 0008 rounds the points down to 64 rows for
its chunked KDA kernels), so the points are exact; GlmEngine.grid = 0 is the hook a grid would set. The setting is
off unless TF_GLM_SHARED_PREFIX is set (the recipe's config turns it on); unset, the kept states, replies and cached
counts are the ones before. cap_victim is unconditional, as in the recipe; the single-stream engine keeps its
oldest-first cap, as the recipe does. The rank messages carry the two slots either way (zeros when off).

On llama-benchy's shape ([system: C, user: "."], then [system: C, user: P]) the second request now reports
cached = the tokens before its first <|user|> ([gMASK]<sop><|system|> and C), with the reply it gets served alone.
Under --parallel each kept state's KDA state and DFlash2 window come out of TF_GLM_CACHE_GIB, so raising
TF_GLM_CACHE_ENTRIES (the recipe keeps 32) wants a larger TF_GLM_CACHE_GIB too.

Tests: tests/test_glm_shared_prefix.py (CPU: the setting, shared_points, user_opener, cap_victim's order, saved
DFlash2 windows; the single-stream engine on both ranks; the multi-stream engine at two ranks, at world 1 over the
fake AFD wire, and with TF_GLM_KV=fp8's rings; replies against the requests served alone; the setting off).

Credit: THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md and the ported functions' comments name MiaAI-Lab and the patches.

